[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Replay.replay","page":"API","text":"replay(instructions::Vector{<:AbstractString}, buf::IO; kwargs...)\n\nReplay the REPL output from given instructions.\n\nEach element of instructions is one complete input submitted to the REPL. Multi-line inputs such as a function ... end definition must stay in a single element. An instruction that the parser reports as incomplete raises an ArgumentError before the child REPL is started. To replay a whole script, pass it as a single string; see the replay(script::AbstractString) method.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nReplay options\n\nuse_ghostwriter: Flag to enable ghostwriter mode. (default: false)\nstartup_prompt: Text to wait for when the child REPL starts. (default: \"julia>\")\nprompt_delimiter: Text ending each subsequent prompt. (default: \"> \") For numbered prompts use startup_prompt=\"In [1]: \", prompt_delimiter=\"]: \", and leave use_ghostwriter=false; ghostwriter assumes the standard prompts.\n\nJulia options\n\njulia_project: Path to a project where julia process runs. (default: @.)\ncmd: Additional command for julia process. (default: \"--color=yes\")\n\nNumbered prompts\n\nInitialize Julia's REPL interface before enabling numbered prompts:\n\nreplay([\"1 + 1\", \"ans * 2\"];\n    cmd=`-q --color=no -e 'using REPL; atreplinit(repl -> (repl.interface = REPL.setup_interface(repl); REPL.numbered_prompt!(repl)))'`,\n    startup_prompt=\"In [1]: \", prompt_delimiter=\"]: \")\n\nInteractive menus\n\nASCII REPL.TerminalMenus menus can be replayed with the default prompt delimiter. Put the request and its response in separate instructions and use a carriage return (\"\\r\") to confirm a selection:\n\nreplay([\n    \"using REPL.TerminalMenus\",\n    \"menu = RadioMenu([\"apple\", \"orange\"]);\",\n    \"choice = request(\"Choose fruit:\", menu)\",\n    \"$(DOWN_ARROW)\\r\",\n    \"@show choice\",\n])\n\nUse \"q\" to cancel a TerminalMenus request. Menu requests must display the configured prompt_delimiter; arbitrary full-screen TUIs or menus with different cursor styles are not automatically detected. Keep use_ghostwriter=false for raw menu keystrokes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Replay.replay"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Replay.replay-Tuple{AbstractString, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API","text":"replay(script::AbstractString, buf::IO=stdout; kwargs...)\n\nSplit script into REPL inputs and replay them.\n\nSplitting uses the REPL's own completeness rule: lines are accumulated until Base.parse_input_line no longer returns an Expr(:incomplete), so a multi-line definition is submitted as one input. Lines that start with ;, ] or ? at column 0 are treated as shell, pkg and help mode commands, and a line starting with a Ctrl-C character interrupts the current mode.\n\nThe script must be complete. If it ends in the middle of an expression, an ArgumentError is raised instead of leaving the child REPL waiting for input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Replay.replay"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Replay.@deparse-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"@deparse expr\n\nReturn expr as a Julia source string using Julia's own printer, i.e. string(expr). LineNumberNodes are removed first, so the result contains no #= file:line =# markers.\n\nThe macro does not try to reproduce the exact whitespace or syntax you typed; it returns Julia's canonical representation of the parsed expression.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> @deparse 1+1\n\"1 + 1\"\n\njulia> @deparse sin(1f0+a)\n\"sin(1.0f0 + a)\"\n\njulia> @deparse f(x) = sin(x)/x\n\"f(x) = begin\\n        sin(x) / x\\n    end\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Replay.@deparse"},{"category":"section","location":"#Replay.jl","page":"Getting started","text":"Replay Julia REPL instructions automatically, including help, package and shell modes. The test suite supports the latest stable Julia (1) and the current long-term support release (lts) on Linux, macOS and Windows. Julia 1.6 is no longer supported.","title":"Replay.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Minimal-example","page":"Getting started","text":"Install Replay with import Pkg; Pkg.add(\"Replay\"), then save this as demo.jl:\n\nusing Replay\n\nreplay([\"x = 1 + 1\", \"println(\\\"Hello from Replay!\\\")\", \"@show x\"];\n       cmd=`-q --color=no`)\n\nRun it with julia --project demo.jl. Replay launches a child Julia REPL; it does not evaluate the instructions in your current session. For package-specific demos, pass julia_project explicitly to replay.\n\nTo capture text instead of displaying it:\n\noutput = replay(\"1 + 1\", IOBuffer(); cmd=`-q --color=no`)\ntext = String(take!(output))\n\nSee the API reference for options, control keys and @deparse.","title":"Minimal example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Replaying-a-script","page":"Getting started","text":"Pass a script as a single string to replay it as a whole:\n\nsrc = read(\"demo.jl\", String)\nreplay(src; cmd=`-q --color=no`)\n\nreplay splits the script the same way the REPL decides that an input is complete: it keeps reading lines while Base.parse_input_line reports an Expr(:incomplete), so a multi-line definition stays one input. A line starting with ;, ] or ? at column 0 switches to shell, pkg or help mode, and an incomplete script raises an ArgumentError before the child REPL starts. The vector form is still available when you want to control each submitted input explicitly: replay([\"x = 1\", \"@show x\"]).","title":"Replaying a script"},{"category":"section","location":"#Recording","page":"Getting started","text":"Replay produces terminal output, not an MP4 file. An external terminal recorder can capture the minimal example without screen recording:\n\nasciinema rec demo.cast --command \"julia --project demo.jl\"\nasciinema play demo.cast\n\nThe full gallery, integration examples and recording/export instructions are maintained separately in ReplayGallery.jl. This manual does not execute or download those examples during its build.","title":"Recording"}]
